<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  An off-ramp that leaves a road before a junction and rejoins where the junction's turn
  would have landed. The turn is offered twice, and on the ground the ramp is islanded off
  from the through lanes, so the junction does not carry it. Left-hand traffic, so a left
  turn is made from the kerbside lane (the highest index; indices run centre-out).

  Four cases, each the shape of one thing the reading has to get right.

  case A, node 1 - the ramp rejoins where the turn lands, so the turn goes

                          13
                          ▲
                          │            way 110, 2 lanes, straight on
       15 ◄──── 12 ◄╌╌╌╌╌╌1
                 ▲        ▲  +90 deg   way 120, 1 lane, the turn
        ramp 130 │        │
        (1 lane) └── 14   │            way 100, 2 lanes, from node 10
                       ╲  │
                        ╲ 10
                          ▲
                          │            way 90, the road before the ramp leaves
                          9

      The ramp leaves node 10 - which is where way 100's own edge starts - and comes out
      at node 12, which is where way 120's edge ends. Both ends match, so 100 -> 120 is
      forbidden and a warning records it. The approach keeps way 110, so nothing is
      stranded, and the two lanes are dealt across 110 alone rather than across 110 and a
      destination that is about to go.

  case B, node 2 - the ramp comes out somewhere else, so nothing is deduced

      Identical to case A except the ramp ends at node 25 instead of node 22. A ramp that
      goes somewhere else is not this turn by another route, and matching it against any
      node of the destination *way* rather than the end of the movement's own edge reads
      six straight-on movements in mosque as bypassed.

  case C, node 3 - a ramp cannot replace a carriageway carrying on

      Way 300 runs straight into way 320 and ramp 350 rejoins at node 32, the far end of
      320's edge. Both ends match, but a ramp replaces a turn, never a road going straight
      ahead, so the movement carries no side and is left alone.

  case D, node 4 - a ramp never leaves a lane with nowhere to go

      A signalised bend: way 400 has one destination, way 420, and ramp 430 rejoins at
      node 42. Removing it would end the lane at the junction, so it stays. A ramp says a
      turn is taken elsewhere, never that a lane has no exit.
-->
